      "name": "PEAKROUTINE",
      "narratives": [
        {
          "heading": "2.1 Thirty seconds — recruiter screen",
          "text": "PeakRoutine is a consumer health app that connects to your wearables — Apple Health, Oura, Fitbit, Garmin, Whoop — and turns that biometric data into a daily picture of your sleep, movement, stress and nutrition. What makes it technically interesting is that it scores everything against your own rolling baseline rather than population averages, detects patterns across multiple metrics at once, and layers Claude on top for meal logging and coaching. It launched at #4 Product of the Day on Product Hunt and is currently in TestFlight early access."
        },
        {
          "heading": "2.2 Ninety seconds — \"tell me about PeakRoutine\"",
          "text": "PeakRoutine is a health and wellness platform built as four pieces: a Flutter app, a Spring Boot API, a Python analytics service, and a self-hosted wearable data platform that normalizes data from seven vendors. The core idea is that population reference ranges are close to useless for wellness. The healthy range for resting heart rate spans roughly 40 to 100 beats per minute, so a single reading tells you almost nothing. Everything in the analytics service is therefore scored against a rolling personal baseline over 30, 60 and 90-day windows, and the signal we look for is coordinated deviation across several metrics rather than any one crossing a line. On top of that there are seven acute physiological pattern detectors — things like a respiratory infection signature, where heart rate decouples from activity while respiratory rate climbs and HRV drops — and eight chronic risk trajectory models that read three-to-six-month trend slopes, where every individual metric can still look normal while the trajectory is adverse. The other half is applied LLM work: a nutrition stack where you can log a meal by searching, scanning a barcode, photographing it, or just describing it in a sentence, all converging on the same food-resolution pipeline. And there is an evaluation harness behind that which compares three ranking strategies with an LLM judge, which is how we discovered the existing heuristic ranker was performing worse than doing no ranking at all."
        },
        {
          "heading": "2.3 Three minutes — hiring manager",
          "text": "Start with the ninety-second version, then continue: The system splits into three services with clear boundaries. Spring Boot owns authentication, business logic, persistence and orchestration. The Python service owns models and inference and is called over HTTP. Keeping those separate means the ML stack can iterate and deploy independently of the core application, which matters because they change at completely different rates — analyzers get tuned weekly, the core API does not. The data platform is the part I would point to for systems design. It replaced device-only, iOS-only, foreground-only HealthKit sync with a webhook-driven pipeline: Svix HMAC verification, TTL-indexed event deduplication, typed event dispatch, provider-normalized mappers, per-metric source-priority resolution, user-local-day bucketing, and idempotent upserts keyed by external ID. Then, because webhooks get dropped, an hourly reconciliation job diffs the vendor's REST aggregates against MongoDB and repairs the difference idempotently. The hardest correctness problem in the whole product is time. Sleep crosses midnight, users travel, wearables report in device-local time, vendors backfill retroactively, and the same day can arrive from three sources at three granularities. Every aggregate depends on getting \"which local day is this?\" right, and a single wrong bucket shifts a chart, breaks a streak and corrupts a correlation. The design decision I would most want to talk about is resolve-at-write versus resolve-at-read for multi-device conflicts. We shipped write-side first because it is cheap and keeps reads simple, but it is lossy — once you pick a winner you cannot revisit it, so when the priority table changed, historical data stayed wrong. The rebuild retains every source's raw contribution and resolves deterministically at read time, which means a priority change re-materializes history. And on the AI side, the thing I would emphasize is that safety is architectural. The product detects disease-adjacent signatures and is explicitly not a medical device, so rather than telling a model to be careful, every user-facing pattern message routes through a module that enforces non-diagnostic language: never diagnose, suggest actions rather than treatments, state what to monitor and when to seek care. A prompt instruction is advisory; a routing layer is a control."
        }
      ],
